// Families: component trees whose members are not interchangeable widgets,
// where a container accepts only its own family's members.
//
// A canvas and its shapes are one; rich text (flows of inline runs) is
// another. Nothing here knows what a shape is: the compiler compares
// declarations.
//
// A family is declared in `sngl:build` -- `component shape build.family` --
// and this package is the rest of the vocabulary. A component says which
// family it belongs to by naming it in its return position, and a slot says
// which one it accepts by naming it as the slot's type. How many children it
// accepts is the wrapper: bare is any number, `family.one[T]` exactly one,
// `option[T]` none or one -- counted as the nodes the population renders, so a
// `for` in a slot of one is refused. How many times a body inserts the slot is
// not bounded.
//
// Naming nothing asks: a component whose return position is empty joins the
// family its body renders, and one whose body renders members of two families
// or of none is asked to say which. A component that takes whatever family it
// is placed in says so with a type parameter, `component effect[F any](…) F` -- a
// lifetime bracket, which sits in a drawing as readily as in a layout -- and
// its body may render only what is generic in F the same way: another such
// component, or a slot of F. A wrapper's call site binds F from the children
// it supplies, `component boundary[T any](content ...component T) T`.
//
// A placement that crosses a family says so where it is written:
// `#[family.crosses]` on a node or a slot insertion lets that one stand where
// its family is not the one accepted, and checks everything else about it as
// ever. It is how a platform's primitive stands in for a node of another
// family, and how a program's own family member shows a widget.
